{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793307398,"nonce":"/9aeW0dZ7ZZznGUDpjJZ22utxTk=","message":"Implementation seam identified while the balancing experiment builds on the NUC: LLVM 21 GlobalValue::materialize() is available through C++, and Ash already builds small C++ bridges in crates/ash/cpp via build_wasm_exception_shim. A narrow bridge can report materialization failures as LLVM-owned messages. LLVMGetBitcodeModuleInContext2 takes ownership of its memory buffer on success, unlike the current eager parse; a Rust conversion must not double-drop the buffer, and the borrowed stream must outlive the module.\n\nCompute body-size, shield and reference summaries once before serializing, then use them to choose owned bodies plus the transitive small-body imports. Materialize those, explicitly turn excluded bodies into declarations before a pass can call materializeAll, and preserve constant/global folding dependencies and personalities. Merely exchanging the parse function while still scanning instruction counts or letting the optimizer materialize everything would not address the issue. BlockAddress/metadata-driven materialization needs a regression or a conservative fallback.\n\nThis is a design follow-up, not an implementation or measured gain. Balancing issue 1cf21d8 is being validated first; compilation measurements now use the NUC at the user's request.","files":null}]}